  "_tier_notes": "price_level = max_level - 3 through tier 5, which is the curve tiers 3-5 already drew and is calibrated so a trash item's mean value stays ~1.55x the coin a humanoid of the same level drops (coin.per_level * level). Tier 5 sat at max_level 999 while its price_level of 33 said 36: a placeholder from before 30+ content existed, which flattened levels 30-60 into one tier. Tiers 6-8 then ACCELERATE above that line (46/58/74 against the 40/47/57 the rule would give) because the rule alone left the 41-49 zones paying less per kill than Southern Stranglethorn at 38-42: those zones are 10-40% coin-droppers against Stranglethorn's 60%, and a coin mob pays every kill while a trash mob pays on 30% of them. Raising the top tiers is the lever that closes that without touching trash_chance.",
  "icon_bands": [
    {
      "max_level": 29,
      "suffix": ""
    },
    {
      "max_level": 43,
      "suffix": "_t2"
    },
    {
      "max_level": 999,
      "suffix": "_t3"
    }
  ],
  "_trash_notes": "min_level / max_level gate an entry to part of the game: the premium entries (no_adjective, min_level 37) are things only deep content drops, and the cheap scraps retire at 36 so a level 50 demon stops dropping Tattered Rags. icon_bands maps a level to an art suffix - the same list tools/reskin_vendor_trash_icons.py reskins against, so art and data cannot disagree. Premium weights are deliberately high - they are ~35-40% of a high-level roll, not a rare treat - because they are the only lever raising high-level income: Kyle ruled out trash_chance (\"We dont need trash chance we need new vendor trash\"), so value per drop has to carry it."
}
